course: world
lesson: 72
title: Insects and spiders
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An insect's body has three parts: the head with its eyes and antennae, the thorax where all six legs and the wings are fixed, and the abdomen, where food is digested.
explain: Insects have no lungs: air flows into their bodies through tiny holes along their sides and travels down thin tubes, which works only in small bodies, and that is one reason insects stay small.
explain: An insect's hard outer skeleton cannot stretch, so a growing insect must moult: it splits its old skin and crawls out in a new, soft one that hardens later.
explain: Spiders are arachnids, like scorpions and ticks: two body parts, eight legs, no antennae and no wings. They make silk in special organs at the end of the abdomen, called spinnerets.
explain: For example, a garden spider builds a sticky web and waits, while a jumping spider spins no web at all: it stalks its prey with its big front eyes and leaps on it like a tiny cat.
fact: An insect's body has a head, a thorax and an abdomen.
fact: An insect's legs and wings are all fixed to the thorax.
fact: Insects smell and feel with their antennae.
fact: Many insects have compound eyes made of hundreds or thousands of tiny lenses, very good at seeing movement all around them.
fact: Insects breathe through tiny holes along their bodies, not through lungs.
fact: Insects and spiders must moult their hard outer skeleton to grow.
fact: There are more known kinds of insects than of all other animals together, and beetles are the biggest group.
fact: Honeybees, ants and termites live in large colonies with a queen who lays the eggs.
fact: A honeybee does a waggle dance to tell other bees where to find flowers.
fact: Spiders are arachnids, together with scorpions, ticks and mites.
fact: Spiders make silk with spinnerets, but not all spiders build webs.
fact: Most spiders have eight eyes, and very few are dangerous to people.
exercise: What are the three parts of an insect's body? | Head, thorax and abdomen | Head, legs and tail | Head, wings and a hard shell | Neck, chest and tail
exercise: To which part of an insect are the legs and wings fixed? | The thorax | The head | The abdomen | The antennae
exercise: What does an insect use its antennae for? | Smelling and feeling | Breathing in air | Catching and biting food | Hearing only
exercise: What is a compound eye? | An eye made of many tiny lenses | Two eyes joined into one | An eye that sees only at night | An eye on the end of an antenna
exercise: How does an insect breathe? | Through tiny holes along its body | With two small lungs inside its chest | With gills on its sides like a fish | Through its mouth only, like a dog panting
exercise: Why must an insect moult to grow? | Its hard outer skeleton cannot stretch | It gets too dirty to see | Its old skin becomes too warm in the summer sun | It needs new colours each year
exercise: Which is the biggest group of insects? | Beetles | Butterflies | Ants | Flies
exercise: Who lays the eggs in a beehive? | The queen | All the worker bees | The male drones | The oldest bees
exercise: Why does a honeybee do a waggle dance? | To show other bees where flowers are | To warm up before flying | To scare away wasps | To clean the pollen off its legs before going in
exercise: Which animals are arachnids, like spiders? | Scorpions, ticks and mites | Beetles, ants and bees | Crabs, shrimps and lobsters | Worms, snails and slugs
exercise: Where does a spider make its silk? | In its spinnerets at the end of the abdomen | In its mouth, like spit | In its eight legs | In its eyes
exercise: How does a jumping spider catch its prey? | It stalks and leaps on it | It waits in a sticky web | It stings it with its tail | It traps it under water
exercise: How many eyes do most spiders have? | Eight | Two | Six | One
exercise: What does a spider have that an insect does not? | Spinnerets for making silk | Antennae on its head | Three body parts | Wings on its back
test: A child finds an empty, insect-shaped skin on a tree trunk. What most likely happened? | An insect moulted and crawled out to grow | An insect died and its soft body melted away | A bird ate the insect that was hiding inside | Rain washed the insect right out of its skin
test: A bee lands on a flower and then flies back to the hive. Soon many bees fly to that flower. How did they find it? | The first bee danced to show them the way | They followed the smell of the hive | They saw it from inside the hive | The queen guessed where it was and sent them there
test: Why could an insect not grow as big as a dog today? | Its breathing tubes could not bring enough air to a body so big | It would be too heavy to fly to flowers | Its antennae would be too long to carry | Its eyes would not work in a big body
test: A small animal has two body parts, no antennae and eight legs, and it hunts on the ground without a web. What is it? | A spider | An ant | A beetle | A fly
test: Which is true about all spiders? | They can make silk | They all build webs | They all have six legs | They are all dangerous to people
test: Why is it hard to swat a fly? | Its compound eyes see movement all around it | It can smell your hand from far away | It can hear your heartbeat as your hand comes near | It feels the heat of your hand
test: A tick sucks blood from a dog. Which group does it belong to? | Arachnids, like spiders | Insects, like fleas | Worms, like leeches | Crustaceans, like crabs
test: If you could count every kind of animal on the Earth, which group would have the most kinds? | Insects | Mammals | Birds | Fish
test: An ant colony has thousands of ants but only one of them lays eggs. Which one? | The queen | The biggest soldier | The youngest worker | Every ant in turn
test: how come the spider in the shed has no web, is it broken | Not all spiders build webs | It ate its own web by mistake | Spiders only build webs in summer | The web is there but always invisible
test: why do bees wiggle about when they get back to the hive | They do a waggle dance to show the others where flowers are | They are shaking the pollen off their legs | They are warming up after a long cold flight | They are dancing to wake up the queen
test: Mia says spiders are insects cos they're creepy crawlies. true? | No, spiders are arachnids, like scorpions, ticks and mites | Yes, every small crawling animal is an insect | Yes, but only the spiders that build webs | No, spiders are a kind of small crab that has moved onto dry land
test: my cousin reckons insects breathe with tiny lungs. right? | No, insects breathe through tiny holes along their bodies | Yes, every insect has two very small lungs | Yes, but only insects that can fly | No, insects breathe only through their antennae
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the body of an insect? | It consists of a head, a thorax and an abdomen | It consists of a head and a single body part | It consists of a head, a thorax and two abdomens | It consists of a thorax and an abdomen without head
exam: Identify the means by which insects breathe. | Through tiny holes along their bodies | Through a pair of lungs | Through gills tucked away behind the head | Through the antennae alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of spiders. | They are arachnids, together with scorpions, ticks and mites | They are insects, together with ants and beetles | They are crustaceans, together with crabs | They are worms, together with earthworms
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the waggle dance performed by a honeybee. | To communicate to other bees the location of flowers | To frighten away hungry birds | To warm the hive in winter | To choose the queen of the colony
